      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We recently reported that antioxidant supplementation with vitamins C and E mitigated ozone related decline in forced expiratory flow (FEF(25-75)) in 158 asthmatic children in an area with high ozone exposure in Mexico City."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A study was undertaken to determine whether deletion of glutathione S-transferase M1 (GSTM1 null genotype), a gene involved in response to oxidative stress, influences ozone related decline in FEF(25-75) and the benefit of antioxidant supplementation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "GSTM1 null children receiving placebo had significant ozone related decrements in FEF(25-75) (percentage change per 50 ppb of ozone 2.9 (95% CI -5.2 to -0.6), p=0.01); GSTM1 positive children did not. Conversely, the effect of antioxidants was stronger in children with the GSTM1 null genotype."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Asthmatic children with a genetic deficiency of GSTM1 may be more susceptible to the deleterious effects of ozone on the small airways and might derive greater benefit from antioxidant supplementation."
        }
      ]},
